AB  - LLM-based coding agents have strong potential for automating software development, but their direct application remains fragile under long and complex requirements. Specification omissions are common, and autonomous local fixes can trigger cascading regressions. In a preliminary experiment, implementing a single business function using a standard specification-driven approach required 65 interaction rounds and approximately 9 hours of manual correction.In this paper, we propose an execution control harness that tightly couples LLM-based code generation with governed execution. Implemented as a YAML-based workflow DSL, the harness integrates mechanically scoped reasoning, deterministic validation gates, automated Git-based rollback, and hybrid human-agent control.A case study on a real-world insurance application of approximately 30 KLOC suggests that the proposed approach helps suppress specification omissions and regression chains. Under comparable conditions, the number of correction iterations was reduced from 65 to 3, and total development time decreased from 9.0 to 0.5 hours. These results indicate that reliable AI-driven software development requires not only capable agents but also execution control architectures that govern the generation process.
ER  - 

