AB  - Development-time knowledge—design rationale, architectural decisions, domain constraints, hard-won lessons—generated during AI-driven software development is largely ephemeral, lost between agent sessions. We propose a knowledge engineering framework whose guiding principle is pervasive AI participation—an aspirational design stance in which the agent accompanies the development workflow as broadly as practical and externalizes what it observes into machine-readable artifacts. The framework casts an Effective Agent as Foundation Model + (Harness ∥ Guardrail): two parallel operational concerns—Harness (course-setting; Knowledge, Skills, Stakeholder personas, Runtime/loader) and Guardrail (deviation detection; static analysis, automated tests, an adversarial review-gate drawing on a heterogeneous foundation-model family (untested), post-push review). A separate, external meta-process, AI-Native Knowledge DevOps (Capture/Refine/Create/Apply, GitOps-versioned, agent-loop driven), refines them across sessions: capture Guardrail findings and re-inject refined artifacts into both Harness and Guardrail. Adopting ADRs, GitOps, and concurrent harness-engineering corpora (AGENTS.md, plans, post-integration sensors, AI-rule promotion [1]–[3]) as foundations rather than novelty, our narrower contribution is a four-cell Harness, a Guardrail panel at parity, and a dual-target refinement loop (a design hypothesis we present, not an evaluated effect). A 142-day, 297-commit longitudinal experience report (a marketsizing tool for business planning under the TAM/SAM/SOM framework, built with Claude Code plus an OpenAI Codex CLI review-gate) illustrates the framework: 9,441 LOC, 441 tests, 14 knowledge documents (7,349 lines), and 92 issues (78 filed via the agent’s issue-lifecycle Skill). Commit-history analysis shows 63% of commits on the agent-loaded artifacts are maintenance-class rather than purely additive—we report this as a descriptive number, not as evidence the cycle caused the share, since the case is N=1 with the developer overlapping the framework authors.
ER  - 

