AB  - AI coding assistants now write a large share of production software, and they write it fast. This shifts software engineering from writing code to governing it: deciding who is accountable for AI-generated behavior, how divergence from intent is detected, and how humans and models divide the labor of oversight. Most teams lack a structured answer, and AI-generated code reaches production carrying security defects, functional bugs, and behavioral drift. We present a specification-driven governance framework that treats the specification as the primary governance artifact and the shared contract for human–AI collaboration. The framework adds a specification review gate modeled on the high-level and low-level design (HLD/LLD) reviews teams already trust; it then treats the approved specification, HLD, and LLD as an explicit, auditable baseline against which generated code is checked for drift, routing every flagged drift to a human for reconciliation. We make the collaboration concrete—defining author, reviewer, model, and reconciler roles as a responsibility assignment (RACI) and specifying the audit trail the baseline produces—and align the control points to the NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001, and the EU AI Act. We evaluate with a controlled within-subject human study of five reviewers performing baseline-anchored versus code-only drift review on real AI-generated services, and—to check robustness in a separate population—a large automated-reviewer replication (three models, 90 machine reviews) of the same design. The human study reports an honest, sharp result: an approved specification did not increase the number of drifts reviewers caught (recall 0.53 vs $0.52, \mathrm{p}=0.69$), but it made findings attributable—reviewers tied 81% of findings to a named requirement versus 0% without a baseline $(\mathrm{p}=0.043)$—and raised confidence, at a significant effort cost $(\approx 48 vs 27 min, p = 0.043)$. The automated replication produced a similar pattern. Recall remained essentially unchanged $(\mathrm{p}=0.95)$, whereas attribution increased from 0.00 to 0.67 (p < 10-4) for all models. We observed this result across reviewers rather than for only one reviewer, suggesting that the effect is associated with the review setup rather than an individual reviewer's behavior. The anonymized artifact released with the study includes the complete set of materials.
ER  - 

